 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfNFZEMAnuK/",
 "title": "How to Answer Salary Expectations in Job Interviews",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains that candidates should avoid leading with their current pay or lowest acceptable number when asked about salary expectations. Instead, candidates should ask for the job's pay range first and use a ChatGPT prompt to practice responses if the interviewer insists.",
 "key_points": [
  "Do not lead with your current pay or the lowest number you take.",
  "If you state your lowest number first, you place yourself at the bottom of the company's range.",
  "First, ask the interviewer: What's the pay range for this job?",
  "If the interviewer pushes back, have an answer ready.",
  "Make sure the lowest number in your range is a number you would actually accept based on your research.",
  "Use a specific ChatGPT prompt to generate responses: 'I’m interviewing for [job] in [city]. Ask me about my experience and the pay range I’ve researched. Then write 2 short answers to ‘What are your salary expectations?’ One asks for the company’s range. The other gives my range if they insist. Don’t mention my current pay.'",
  "Practice both answers out loud before naming a range."
 ],
 "content": "When an interviewer asks what are your salary expectations? Don't lead with your current pay or the lowest number you take. So imagine you're hoping for 60k, but the jobs range is 60 to 90 k. If you say 60 k first, then you're putting yourself at the bottom of the range. Don't do that. Instead, what you wanna do is ask what's the pay range for this job? And if they push back, make sure you have an answer ready. So paste this in a chat to help you. I'm interviewing for [job] in [city]. Ask me about my experience and the pay range I've researched. Then write 2 short answers to What are your salary expectations? One asks for the company's range. The other gives my range if they insist. Don't mention my current pay. Ask me about my experience and the pay range I researched. Then write two short answers to what are your salary expectations? One asks for the company's range, the other gives my range. If they insist, but don't mention my current pay, then what you wanna do is practice both answers out loud before you name a range. By the way, make sure you'd actually accept the lowest number. Text on screen: When an interviewer asks what are your salary expectations? Don't lead with your current pay or the lowest number you take. So imagine you're hoping for 60 k, but the jobs range is 60 to 90 k. If you say 60 k first, then you're putting yourself at the bottom of the range. Don't do that. Instead, what you wanna do is ask what's the pay range for this job? And if they push back, make sure you have an answer ready. So paste this in a chat to help you I'm interviewing for [job] in [city]. Ask me about my experience and the pay range I've researched. Then write 2 short answers to 'What are your salary expectations?' One asks for the company's range. The other gives my range if they insist. Don't mention my current pay. Ask me about my experience and the pay range I researched. Then write two short answers to what are your salary expectations? One asks for the company's range, the other gives my range. If they insist, but don't mention my current pay, then what you wanna do is practice both answers out loud before you name a range. By the way, make sure you'd actually accept the lowest number.",
